---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Chords & actions" description="How six10nine chords are played, and what a chord can do in each app." docs>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1>Chords &amp; actions</h1>
  <p class="lede">A chord is ⌘ plus keys. What it does is up to you, app by app.</p>

  <h2>Playing chords</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>How</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Chord</td>
          <td>Hold ⌘ and press keys together, like the notes of a piano chord. Add ⇧, ⌥ or ⌃ if you like.</td>
          <td><kbd>⌘8</kbd> <kbd>⌘8K</kbd> <kbd>⌘⇧8K</kbd></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>In order</td>
          <td>Hold ⌘ and press keys one after another. Order and repeats count.</td>
          <td><kbd>⌘P·O·O·P</kbd></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <kbd>⌘P·O·O·P</kbd>, <kbd>⌘P·O·P</kbd> and the chord <kbd>⌘OP</kbd> are three different chords, and each can have
    its own action. A chord finishes when you release ⌘ or pause, or straight away when nothing longer could follow.
  </p>
  <p>
    <kbd>⌘8</kbd> and <kbd>⌘0</kbd> work from the first launch. The menu bar menu lists the chord keys you can play. A ⌘
    shortcut that isn’t a chord reaches the app as usual, so <kbd>⌘C</kbd> still copies.
  </p>

  <h2>Actions</h2>
  <p>
    Each app keeps its own action for each chord. An action is a <strong>menu item</strong>, which six10nine clicks for
    you through Accessibility. Menu items keep working when you change an app’s shortcuts. If the item is gone or
    disabled, six10nine beeps and does nothing. Chords never press keys in the app for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you record a shortcut, six10nine saves the menu item in that app that shows it. If no menu item shows that
    shortcut, it asks you to pick a menu item instead. A chord with no action in the current app is handed to the app as
    if six10nine weren’t there, unless <a href="/ai">AI matching</a> finds an equivalent.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chords recorded as shortcuts by earlier versions are looked up in the app’s menus the next time you play them and
    switched to the menu item that shows that shortcut. If there isn’t one, the chord does nothing and six10nine tells
    you to record it again.
  </p>

  <h2>Recording</h2>
  <p>Hold <kbd>⌘67</kbd> for a second in the app you want.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Keys</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Chord</td><td>⌘ + keys</td><td>Plays the chord. Playing again replaces it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td></td><td><kbd>↩</kbd></td><td>Uses this chord and moves on.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Action</td><td>A shortcut with ⌘, ⌃ or ⌥, or a key like F5</td><td>Picks the menu item that shows it. If none does, six10nine asks you to pick a menu item instead.</td></tr>
        <tr><td></td><td>Typing</td><td>Searches the app’s menu items; every word must appear in the menu path.</td></tr>
        <tr><td></td><td><kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd></td><td>Moves through the top eight matches.</td></tr>
        <tr><td></td><td><kbd>↩</kbd></td><td>Saves.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Any</td><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Cancels.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Return, Esc, plain arrows and plain ⌫ drive the recording, so they can’t be recorded as actions or used in chords.
    Keys are named by their physical position, so ⌥ and ⇧ don’t change what a key is called.
  </p>

  <h2>Reserved gestures</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Gesture</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Hold <kbd>⌘67</kbd> for 1 s</td><td>Record a chord.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>⌘67</kbd> while disabled</td><td>Turns chords back on, if that option is checked.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>⌘69</kbd></td><td>Opens a camera mirror with a dry, deadpan voice assistant. Needs macOS 26 with Apple Intelligence. Try it.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Changing and removing chords</h2>
  <p>
    Record the chord again to replace its action in that app. AI agents can also list, set and remove chords through the
    <a href="/mcp">MCP server</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
